{"subjectId":"python","id":"pattern-matching","title":"Pattern Matching (match)","group":"Getting Started","order":8,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>match</code> (Python 3.10+) compares a value against a series of <em>patterns</em> — shapes, not just values — and binds the pieces it finds to names.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Structural pattern matching</strong> — <code>match subject:</code> followed by <code>case pattern:</code> blocks. Cases are tried top to bottom; the <strong>first</strong> one that matches runs, and there is no fall-through.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i>Literals, OR-patterns, <code>as</code> and the wildcard</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def http_status(code):\n    match code:\n        case 200 | 201 | 204:            # OR-pattern\n            return \"success\"\n        case 301 | 302 as r:             # bind the matched value with as\n            return f\"redirect {r}\"\n        case 404:\n            return \"not found\"\n        case _:                          # wildcard: matches anything, binds nothing\n            return \"other\"\n\nprint(http_status(201), http_status(302))   # success redirect 302\nprint(http_status(404), http_status(500))   # not found other</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-brackets\"></i>Sequence patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def summarize(seq):\n    match seq:\n        case []:\n            return \"empty\"\n        case [x]:\n            return f\"one item: {x}\"\n        case [x, y]:\n            return f\"pair: {x}, {y}\"\n        case [first, *rest]:             # star captures the remainder as a list\n            return f\"{first} and {len(rest)} more\"\n\nprint(summarize([]))              # empty\nprint(summarize([7]))             # one item: 7\nprint(summarize((1, 2)))          # pair: 1, 2   ← tuples match list patterns too\nprint(summarize([1, 2, 3, 4]))    # 1 and 3 more\nprint(summarize(\"ab\"))            # None   ← str is NOT treated as a sequence here</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def fizzbuzz(n):\n    match (n % 3, n % 5):                # match a tuple built on the spot\n        case (0, 0): return \"FizzBuzz\"\n        case (0, _): return \"Fizz\"\n        case (_, 0): return \"Buzz\"\n        case _: return str(n)\n\nprint(\" \".join(fizzbuzz(i) for i in range(1, 16)))   # 1 2 Fizz 4 Buzz Fizz 7 8 Fizz Buzz 11 Fizz 13 14 FizzBuzz</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-braces\"></i>Mapping patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def handle(event):\n    match event:\n        case {\"type\": \"click\", \"x\": x, \"y\": y}:\n            return f\"click at ({x}, {y})\"\n        case {\"type\": \"key\", \"key\": str(k)} if len(k) == 1:\n            return f\"key {k!r}\"\n        case {\"type\": \"resize\", **rest}:\n            return f\"resize {rest}\"\n        case {\"type\": t}:\n            return f\"unhandled {t}\"\n\nprint(handle({\"type\": \"click\", \"x\": 3, \"y\": 4, \"ts\": 99}))   # click at (3, 4)   ← extra keys are ignored\nprint(handle({\"type\": \"key\", \"key\": \"q\"}))                  # key 'q'\nprint(handle({\"type\": \"resize\", \"w\": 800, \"h\": 600}))       # resize {'w': 800, 'h': 600}\nprint(handle({\"type\": \"scroll\"}))                           # unhandled scroll</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Mapping patterns match if the listed keys are present — other keys are allowed. Sequence patterns are the opposite: the length must match exactly unless you use <code>*rest</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Class patterns</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Point:\n    x: int\n    y: int\n\n@dataclass\nclass Circle:\n    center: Point\n    r: float\n\ndef where(shape):\n    match shape:\n        case Point(x=0, y=0):\n            return \"origin\"\n        case Point(0, y):                # positional → uses Point.__match_args__\n            return f\"on the y-axis at {y}\"\n        case Point(x, y) if x == y:      # guard\n            return \"on the diagonal\"\n        case Circle(Point(0, 0), r):     # patterns nest\n            return f\"circle of radius {r} at the origin\"\n        case Point() | Circle():         # just an isinstance check\n            return \"somewhere else\"\n        case _:\n            return \"not a shape\"\n\nprint(where(Point(0, 0)), \"|\", where(Point(0, 5)))        # origin | on the y-axis at 5\nprint(where(Point(3, 3)), \"|\", where(Circle(Point(0, 0), 2)))   # on the diagonal | circle of radius 2 at the origin\nprint(where(Point(1, 2)), \"|\", where(\"hi\"))               # somewhere else | not a shape\nprint(Point.__match_args__)                               # ('x', 'y')</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def kind(value):\n    match value:\n        case bool():                     # check bool before int — bool is a subclass of int\n            return \"bool\"\n        case int(n) if n &lt; 0:            # built-ins match the whole value positionally\n            return \"negative int\"\n        case int() | float():\n            return \"number\"\n        case str() as s:\n            return f\"string of {len(s)}\"\n        case _:\n            return type(value).__name__\n\nprint(kind(True), kind(-4), kind(2.5), kind(\"abc\"), kind(None))   # bool negative int number string of 3 NoneType</code></pre></figure>\n<p>A class pattern <code>Cls(...)</code> first checks <code>isinstance(subject, Cls)</code>, then matches attributes: keyword sub-patterns by name, positional ones via the class's <code>__match_args__</code> tuple (generated automatically by <code>@dataclass</code> and <code>namedtuple</code>).</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-shield-check\"></i>Guards</h3>\n<p><code>case pattern if condition:</code> — the pattern must match <strong>and</strong> the condition must be truthy. Names bound by the pattern are available in the guard.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def classify(pair):\n    match pair:\n        case (a, b) if a == b:\n            return \"equal\"\n        case (a, b) if a &gt; b:\n            return \"descending\"\n        case (_, _):\n            return \"ascending\"\n\nprint(classify((2, 2)), classify((5, 1)), classify((1, 5)))   # equal descending ascending</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>The capture-pattern trap</h3>\n<p>A <strong>bare name</strong> in a pattern is a <em>capture</em>: it matches anything and assigns to that name. To compare against a constant, use a <strong>dotted name</strong> (a value pattern) such as <code>Color.RED</code> or <code>http.HTTPStatus.OK</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>RED = \"red\"\n\ndef is_red(color):\n    match color:\n        case RED:                        # captures! binds a local RED = color\n            return True\n    return False\n\nprint(is_red(\"blue\"))                    # True   ← oops\n\nfrom enum import Enum\nclass Color(Enum):\n    RED = \"red\"\n    BLUE = \"blue\"\n\ndef is_red_fixed(color):\n    match color:\n        case Color.RED:                  # dotted name → compared with ==\n            return True\n        case _:\n            return False\n\nprint(is_red_fixed(Color.BLUE), is_red_fixed(Color.RED))   # False True</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>src = \"match x:\\n    case y:\\n        pass\\n    case 1:\\n        pass\\n\"\ntry:\n    compile(src, \"&lt;demo&gt;\", \"exec\")\nexcept SyntaxError as e:\n    print(e.msg)          # name capture 'y' makes remaining patterns unreachable</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>Pattern kinds cheat sheet</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Example</th><th>Matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Literal</td><td><code>case 0:</code> <code>case \"quit\":</code> <code>case None:</code></td><td>Equal value (<code>None</code>/<code>True</code>/<code>False</code> compared with <code>is</code>)</td></tr><tr><td>Capture</td><td><code>case x:</code></td><td>Anything; binds <code>x</code></td></tr><tr><td>Wildcard</td><td><code>case _:</code></td><td>Anything; binds nothing</td></tr><tr><td>Value</td><td><code>case Color.RED:</code></td><td>Equal to a dotted constant</td></tr><tr><td>OR</td><td><code>case 1 | 2 | 3:</code></td><td>Any alternative</td></tr><tr><td>AS</td><td><code>case [x, y] as pair:</code></td><td>The inner pattern; binds the whole subject too</td></tr><tr><td>Sequence</td><td><code>case [x, *rest]:</code></td><td>Lists/tuples (not <code>str</code>/<code>bytes</code>) of the right length</td></tr><tr><td>Mapping</td><td><code>case {\"id\": i}:</code></td><td>Dicts containing those keys; <code>**rest</code> collects the others</td></tr><tr><td>Class</td><td><code>case Point(x, y=0):</code></td><td><code>isinstance</code> + attribute patterns</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bulb\"></i>When to use <code>match</code></h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Use <code>match</code> to branch on the <strong>shape</strong> of data: parsed commands, JSON events, AST nodes, tuples of states.</p></div><div class=\"callout callout--bad\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Avoid</div><p>Using it as a glorified <code>switch</code> for simple value → value mapping — a <code>dict</code> lookup is shorter and faster.</p></div></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is match different from a C/Java switch?</summary><div><p><code>switch</code> compares one value against constants. <code>match</code> tests <em>structure</em>: it can destructure lists, dicts and objects, bind variables, combine alternatives with <code>|</code>, and filter with guards. There is no fall-through and no <code>break</code> — the first matching case runs and the statement ends.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does case RED: match every value?</summary><div><p>A bare name is a capture pattern: it always matches and binds the subject to <code>RED</code>. Constants must be referenced with a dotted name (<code>Color.RED</code>, <code>module.RED</code>) so Python treats them as value patterns compared with <code>==</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do positional class patterns like Point(0, y) work?</summary><div><p>Python checks <code>isinstance(subject, Point)</code>, then maps positional sub-patterns to attribute names using <code>Point.__match_args__</code> — here <code>('x', 'y')</code> — and matches <code>subject.x</code> against <code>0</code> and binds <code>subject.y</code> to <code>y</code>. Dataclasses and named tuples define <code>__match_args__</code> automatically.</p></div></details></section>"}